  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify constant offset systems by applying the superposition test to a continuous-time input-output rule and recognizing that an added output offset breaks homogeneity and the zero-input, zero-output condition.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Constant Offset Systems",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A constant offset system is a rule of the form $T\\{x(t)\\}=S\\{x(t)\\}+c$, where $c$ is a constant added to the output. The important cue is that the offset does not depend on the input, so it stays present even when the input changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For linearity, you want the superposition checks to work, and you also want the zero-input, zero-output condition to hold. A constant offset is a good place to see the failure quickly because a constant term does not disappear when the input is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{0\\}=S\\{0\\}+c",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{0\\}=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the smallest nontrivial base case, $T\\{x(t)\\}=x(t)+3$, so setting the input to zero gives $T\\{0\\}=0+3=3$. The output is not zero, so the system fails the linearity test immediately.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the pattern to remember: an added constant output term breaks linearity right away, before you even move on to homogeneity or additivity checks.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Checking the Zero-Input Output of a Constant Offset System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate the zero-input output for $y(t)=x(t)+7$. Is the rule linear?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is the zero-input test: set the input to $0$ and see whether the output is also $0$. For a constant-offset system, the added constant stays in the output even when the input disappears.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nT\\{0\\} &= 0 + 7 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The linearity condition requires $T\\{0\\}=0$, but here $T\\{0\\}=7$. That mismatch is enough to classify the rule as nonlinear immediately.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the minimum evidence you need: a nonzero output at zero input already breaks the zero-input, zero-output condition, so the system fails linearity before any further test is necessary.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Testing Homogeneity for an Added Output Offset",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y(t)=2x(t)+1$, test homogeneity using input $x(t)$ scaled by $a=3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the homogeneity test: scale the input by $a$ and compare $T\\{ax(t)\\}$ with $aT\\{x(t)\\}$. Here the constant offset should stay visible, because that is the part that does not scale with the input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{3x\\}=2(3x(t))+1=6x(t)+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3T\\{x\\}=3(2x(t)+1)=6x(t)+3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two expressions do not match: the scaled input gives $6x(t)+1$, but scaling the output gives $6x(t)+3$. The constant term does not scale the same way as the input, so the homogeneity condition fails.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $T\\{3x\\}\\neq 3T\\{x\\}$, the rule is nonlinear. This is the same offset-driven failure you saw in the zero-input test, now shown through scaling.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Testing Additivity for an Added Output Offset",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Check additivity for $y(t)=x(t)+9$ with generic inputs $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the additivity test: compare the output from the summed input with the sum of the two separate outputs. With a constant offset, the same constant appears on each separate output, so this is where the mismatch shows up.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x_1+x_2\\}=x_1+x_2+9",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x_1\\}+T\\{x_2\\}=(x_1+9)+(x_2+9)=x_1+x_2+18",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two sides do not match: the summed input gives $x_1+x_2+9$, but the separate outputs give $x_1+x_2+18$. The constant offset gets counted twice on the right side, so the additivity condition fails.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $T\\{x_1+x_2\\}\\neq T\\{x_1\\}+T\\{x_2\\}$, the rule is nonlinear. This is the same offset-driven failure pattern you saw in the zero-input and homogeneity checks, now shown with two inputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Classifying Otherwise Linear Rules with Constant Offsets",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $y(t)=2x(t-1)+5$ using the superposition test framework.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that this rule looks linear except for the added constant. So the right move is to test the rule as a whole and check whether the offset breaks the linearity conditions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{0\\}=2(0)+5=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The zero-input test already fails, because a linear system must satisfy $T\\{0\\}=0$. Here the output is $5$, so the rule is not linear even before we look at the other superposition checks.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{a x(t)\\}=2a\\,x(t-1)+5,\\qquad aT\\{x(t)\\}=a\\bigl(2x(t-1)+5\\bigr)=2a\\,x(t-1)+5a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The homogeneity comparison shows the same problem: the constant term stays $5$ in $T\\{a x(t)\\}$, but it becomes $5a$ in $aT\\{x(t)\\}$. Since those expressions are different, homogeneity fails too.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nT\\{x_1(t)+x_2(t)\\} &= 2\\bigl(x_1(t-1)+x_2(t-1)\\bigr)+5 \\\\\nT\\{x_1(t)\\}+T\\{x_2(t)\\} &= \\bigl(2x_1(t-1)+5\\bigr)+\\bigl(2x_2(t-1)+5\\bigr) = 2x_1(t-1)+2x_2(t-1)+10\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The additivity check gives the same verdict: the constant appears once on the summed-input side but twice on the separate-output side. The offset is the only reason the rule fails, so the classification is nonlinear due to the added constant term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify constant offset systems by applying the superposition test to a continuous-time input-output rule and recognizing that an added output offset breaks homogeneity and the zero-input, zero-output condition.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Checking the Zero-Input Output of a Constant Offset System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $T\\{x(t)\\}=x(t)-4$, apply the zero-input test. What is $T\\{0\\}$ and what does it imply?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Set $x(t)=0$, then $T\\{0\\}=4$, so the system passes the zero-input condition and is linear.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Set $x(t)=0$, then $T\\{0\\}=-4$. Since $T\\{0\\}\\neq 0$, the zero-input, zero-output condition fails, so the system is nonlinear.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Zero-input output is optional if the system satisfies other parts of the superposition test.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Because of the product $0\\cdot 4$, $T\\{0\\}=0$, so the system is linear.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "The system is linear if it scales outputs correctly, and the zero-input result is $T\\{0\\}=0+4=4$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "B"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "$T\\{0\\}=-4$, so the zero-input, zero-output condition is violated and the system is nonlinear.",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For linearity, a constant-offset rule must satisfy $T\\{0\\}=0$. Substituting $x(t)=0$ gives $T\\{0\\}=0-4=-4$, so the condition fails immediately.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $T\\{x(t)\\}=x(t)+4$, the zero-input check gives what result and what is the right linearity conclusion?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{0\\}=4$, which is nonzero, so the zero-input, zero-output requirement fails and the system is nonlinear.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{0\\}=0$, so this system is linear from the zero-input test.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{0\\}=-4$ because the system doubles the offset sign before subtracting it.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Zero-input output is not checked for constant offsets; it is enough to verify additivity for nonzero inputs.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{0\\}=8$, and the system remains linear because that is a constant output offset.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "$T\\{0\\}=4$, so the rule violates the zero-input, zero-output condition and is nonlinear.",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Substitute zero input directly: $T\\{0\\}=0+4=4$. A linear system requires $T\\{0\\}=0$, so the mismatch is a definitive failure.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Constant Offset Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Testing Homogeneity for an Added Output Offset",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $T\\{x(t)\\}=4x(t)-3$, test homogeneity with scale factor $a=-2$ by comparing $T\\{-2x(t)\\}$ to $-2T\\{x(t)\\}$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{-2x(t)\\}=4(-2x(t))-3=-8x(t)-3$ and $-2T\\{x(t)\\}=-2(4x(t)-3)=-8x(t)+6$, so the constants do not match and homogeneity fails.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{-2x(t)\\}=4(-2x(t))+(-3)(-2)=-8x(t)+6$ and $-2T\\{x(t)\\}=-8x(t)-3$, so the system is homogeneous.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{-2x(t)\\}=4(-2x(t))-3=-2x(t)-3$ and $-2T\\{x(t)\\}=-8x(t)+6$, so this is a linear scaling mismatch from the $x(t)$ term.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Because the offset is constant, any affine output rule is homogeneous, so no comparison is needed.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "To test homogeneity it is enough to check only $T\\{0\\}\\neq 0$, so this confirms the system is linear.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "Choice $A$.",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Replace $x(t)$ by $-2x(t)$ and compute $T\\{-2x(t)\\}=4(-2x(t))-3=-8x(t)-3$. Then scale the original output: $-2T\\{x(t)\\}=-2\\bigl(4x(t)-3\\bigr)=-8x(t)+6$. The constants differ, so $T\\{-2x\\}\\neq -2T\\{x\\}$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $T\\{x(t)\\}=6x(t)+5$, test homogeneity at $a=-3$ and pick the correct comparison result.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{-3x(t)\\}=6(-3x(t))+5=-18x(t)+5$ and $-3T\\{x(t)\\}=-3(6x(t)+5)=-18x(t)-15$, which do not match.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T\\{-3x(t)\\}=6(-3x(t))-15=-18x(t)-15$ and $-3T\\{x(t)\\}=-18x(t)-15$, which shows homogeneity holds.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
